[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m assistant
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m [response] To solve the problem, we need to understand the geometric properties of the complex numbers involved. Let's denote the complex number \( z \) in polar form as \( z = re^{i\theta} \). Since the distance Eli travels from his starting point to the last house is \(\sqrt{2012}\) on both days, we can infer that the magnitude of \( z \) is 1 (i.e., \( r = 1 \)). Therefore, \( z \) can be written as \( e^{i\theta} \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m ### Step 1: Analyze the Saturday Delivery
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m On Saturday, Eli starts at \( z \) and delivers milk to houses at \( z^3, z^5, z^7, \ldots, z^{2013} \). The last house he visits is at \( z^{2013} \). The distance from \( z \) to \( z^{2013} \) is given by:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m |z^{2013} - z| = |e^{i2013\theta} - e^{i\theta}|
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m Since \( |z| = 1 \), we have:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m |e^{i2013\theta} - e^{i\theta}| = \sqrt{2012}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m ### Step 2: Analyze the Sunday Delivery
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m On Sunday, Eli starts at 1 and delivers milk to houses at \( z^2, z^4, z^6, \ldots, z^{2012} \). The last house he visits is at \( z^{2012} \). The distance from 1 to \( z^{2012} \) is given by:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m |z^{2012} - 1| = |e^{i2012\theta} - 1|
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m Since \( |z| = 1 \), we have:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m \[
