[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m First, we use the fact that the sum of the squares of the distances from any point to the vertices of a regular octahedron is constant. This is a known geometric property of regular octahedrons. Let's denote the distance from \(P\) to the sixth vertex as \(d\). The sum of the squares of the distances from \(P\) to all six vertices is the same for any point \(P\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m Let's denote the distances from \(P\) to the vertices as \(a, b, c, d, e, f\). We know five of these distances: \(a = 3\), \(b = 7\), \(c = 8\), \(d = 9\), and \(e = 11\). We need to find \(f\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m The sum of the squares of the distances is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m \[ a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 + e^2 + f^2 = \text{constant} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
