[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m Given:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m - The mass of the softball (\(m_{\text{softball}}\)) is 200 g.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m - The mass of the baseball (\(m_{\text{baseball}}\)) is 150 g.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m - Jen and Jack apply the same force to their respective balls.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m Let's denote:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m - The force applied by Jen and Jack as \(F\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m - The acceleration of the softball as \(a_{\text{softball}}\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m - The acceleration of the baseball as \(a_{\text{baseball}}\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m From Newton's second law, we have:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m \[ F = m_{\text{softball}} \cdot a_{\text{softball}} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m \[ F = m_{\text{baseball}} \cdot a_{\text{baseball}} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m Since the force \(F\) is the same for both balls, we can set the two equations equal to each other:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m \[ m_{\text{softball}} \cdot a_{\text{softball}} = m_{\text{baseball}} \cdot a_{\text{baseball}} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m Substituting the given masses:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m \[ 200 \cdot a_{\text{softball}} = 150 \cdot a_{\text{baseball}} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m To find the ratio of the accelerations, we solve for \(a_{\text{softball}} / a_{\text{baseball}}\):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m \[ \frac{a_{\text{softball}}}{a_{\text{baseball}}} = \frac{150}{200} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
