[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m Given:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m - The mass of the softball (\(m_{\text{softball}}\)) is 200 g, which is 0.2 kg.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m - The mass of the baseball (\(m_{\text{baseball}}\)) is 150 g, which is 0.15 kg.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m - The force applied by Jen and Jack is the same.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m Let's denote the force as \(F\), the acceleration of the softball as \(a_{\text{softball}}\), and the acceleration of the baseball as \(a_{\text{baseball}}\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m From the formula \(F = ma\), we can express the accelerations as:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m \[ a_{\text{softball}} = \frac{F}{m_{\text{softball}}} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m \[ a_{\text{baseball}} = \frac{F}{m_{\text{baseball}}} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
