[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m Let the vertices of the octahedron be \(A, B, C, D, E, F\), and the distances from \(P\) to these vertices be \(d_A, d_B, d_C, d_D, d_E, d_F\). We know five of these distances: 3, 7, 8, 9, and 11. Let the sixth distance be \(x\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m The sum of the squares of the distances from \(P\) to the vertices is the same for any point \(P\) in space relative to the octahedron. Therefore, we can write:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m \[d_A^2 + d_B^2 + d_C^2 + d_D^2 + d_E^2 + d_F^2 = 3^2 + 7^2 + 8^2 + 9^2 + 11^2 + x^2\]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m The sum of the squares of the known distances is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m \[3^2 + 7^2 + 8^2 + 9^2 + 11^2 = 9 + 49 + 64 + 81 + 121 = 324\]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m So, we have:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m \[324 + x^2 = 324 + x^2\]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m Since the sum of the squares is constant, we can equate the sum of the squares of the known distances to the sum of the squares of all distances:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m \[324 + x^2 = 324 + x^2\]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m This simplifies to:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m \[x^2 = 324 - 324 + x^2 = 324 - 324 + 36 = 36\]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1412833)[0m 
